module.exports="## FACTS ABOUT KALYAN (stated by Kalyan himself; highest priority)\n- Name: Kalyan Sudhakar (screen name \"Gomma\" for his art/visual work).\n- Based in Tempe, Arizona, USA. Open to relocation. Also open to remote work.\n- Currently works at Mercor (design expert training AI models), and has done design-expert contract work for AI labs (see resume).\n- What Kalyan is working on now: he is shipping Keep x Meta. The product and application are built and launched; he and the team keep testing it with users, and among themselves, to scale it. Link: work/keep/.\n- Roles he fits: design engineer, UX designer, product designer, doodle artist; lately focused on AI design.\n- Recently trains AI models with his design expertise: refining models so they produce better designs across STEM and other use cases, including design systems.\n- Domains he has worked in: immersive (XR) experiences, AI product design, AI explainability, enterprise UX, healthcare customer experience, social media applications, system design with Procreate.\n- Research interests: accessibility and learning experiences.\n- Work authorization / visa / sponsorship questions: answer with this fact only, never starting with yes or no, and never saying he is on the STEM extension: \"Kalyan is currently on an F-1 visa under OPT, and he is eligible for the STEM extension of 2 more years.\" Do not add anything about future sponsorship unless the visitor asks.\n- Availability: 7 AM to 7 PM Arizona time (the site shows an Available or Away status for this); quite flexible on weekends.\n- Job market: actively looking for full-time roles. Open to collaborating on a startup or venture.\n- Start date: can start immediately.\n- Contact: kalyansudhakar25@gmail.com, +1 (602) 614 7075.\n- Ideation method: he uses the Double Diamond (Discover, Define, Develop, Deliver): research broadly, define the right problem, explore solutions, then refine and ship. When asked how he ideates or what his process is, explain it briefly and append the exact token [[diamond]] at the end of the answer so the page shows the diagram.\n- Links: LinkedIn https://www.linkedin.com/in/kalyan-sudhakar/ ; X https://x.com/Kalyan0025 ; Instagram https://www.instagram.com/kalyandotcom/ ; GitHub https://github.com/Kalyan0025 ; r\u00e9sum\u00e9 (PDF) https://drive.google.com/file/d/1_pNTEsNna9EJru77OIiJ-USz-itfv_lM/view?usp=sharing . Mention LinkedIn and the word r\u00e9sum\u00e9 by name so the page can link them.\n\n## RESUME (recruiter view)\nRecruiter view\n Kalyan Sudhakar\n AI Product Designer. UX Research and Product Design , M.S. HCI at Arizona State University.\n kalyansudhakar25@gmail.com\n+1 (602) 614 7075 \u00b7 Tempe, AZ\n Skills\n UX Research Product Design AI Product Design Prompt Engineering Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) Immersive Experience Design Explainable AI (XAI) Design Systems Accessibility (WCAG 2.2) Design-to-Code (React \u00b7 Next.js) Human-Centered Design Information Architecture User Flows Journey Mapping Wireframing Responsive Design Usability Testing Heuristic Evaluation Contextual Inquiry User Interviews Task Analysis Persona Development Research Synthesis A/B Testing Survey Analysis Component Libraries Design Tokens Atomic Design Brand Systems Visual Hierarchy Typography Color Theory Layout Design Editorial Design Micro-Interactions Interaction Patterns Inclusive Design Conversational UX Human-in-the-Loop Evaluation Vision-Language Model Data AI Workflow Design Trust-Centered Design Figma Figma Make FigJam Adobe Creative Cloud Framer ProtoPie Miro Cursor Claude Code RL Studio Airtable Taiga HTML5 CSS3 JavaScript TypeScript Tailwind CSS WebGL REST APIs Storybook Git \n Summary\n Designer with 3+ years of experience designing web, SaaS, AI, XR, and enterprise products. Built 450+ reusable components and created user flows, wireframes, prototypes, and design specifications for complex workflows. Conducted user research and usability testing to improve usability and task completion. Currently trains and evaluates AI design-generation and vision-language models through prompt engineering , RLHF , and human-in-the-loop design QA. Worked with Figma, Framer, ProtoPie, and AI-assisted design tools to improve design consistency and support faster product delivery.\n Experience\n Mercor\n Design Expert (Visual & Prompt Engineering) \u00b7 Remote (Tempe, AZ)\n Jul 2026 \u2013 Present\n Multimodal visual modeling: Structure complex data into high-precision diagrams, plots, and technical figures for vision-language model (VLM) training ; design domain-specific visual prompts and evaluate model interpretation to improve visual reasoning and data extraction, verifying quantitative accuracy in RL Studio.\n Prompt engineering & RLHF: Author multi-turn prompts and golden-standard reference responses that tune LLM and AI-assistant reasoning, instruction-following, and constraint adherence.\n Multimodal dataset curation: Curate, categorize, and metadata-tag creative design artifacts for foundation-model training; stress-test generation against edge cases to surface hallucinations and output inconsistencies, tracked in Taiga.\n Design QA & HITL evaluation: Audit AI-generated UI layouts and visual assets against UX heuristics, typography, visual hierarchy, and WCAG criteria, delivering ground-truth scoring and benchmark evaluations that improve design-generation model fidelity and alignment across high-throughput Airtable review pipelines.\n Taste Labs (Taste Tech Inc.)\n Design Expert (Contract) \u00b7 Remote\n Aug 2026 \u2013 Oct 2026\n Engineered detailed design prompts specifying design tokens, component systems, layout, and copy to build and test 50+ end-to-end products across web apps, websites, mobile apps, design systems, and data-visualization interfaces for a frontier AI lab; diagnosed model failure modes and fixed outputs to production polish, feeding corrections back as RLHF preference and critique data.\n Built bespoke brand systems (typography, color, imagery, layout, design language) and extended them into Figma presentation and editorial assets as benchmark-quality references; ran pairwise, rubric-based QA on AI-generated interfaces covering visual hierarchy, prompt fidelity, WCAG 2.2 AA , heuristic evaluation, and generic-pattern detection, with written design rationales.\n Mesh Labs\n XR Creative \u00b7 Tempe, AZ\n Sep 2025 \u2013 May 2026\n Designed complex AI/XR, CMS/admin, and web workflows by translating user needs and functional constraints into accessible user flows, wireframes, prototypes, and implementation-ready specs.\n Built 2 design systems and 40+ reusable components with tokens, responsive rules, accessibility states, motion guidance, templates, and documentation for scalable product delivery.\n Used Figma Make, Cursor, Claude Code, usability reviews, and design QA to improve interface consistency by 30% , reduce navigation friction by 24% , and cut engineering iteration cycles by 35% .\n Siemens\n Product Designer \u00b7 India\n Apr 2022 \u2013 Jul 2024\n Architected a comprehensive design system with 450+ reusable components and design tokens in Figma, reducing UI development time by 40% across 12 product teams through Atomic Design methodology.\n Orchestrated user research synthesis from 85+ contextual inquiries and usability testing sessions with industrial engineers, increasing task completion rates by 35% for complex energy management workflows.\n Designed and prototyped an AI-assisted predictive maintenance interface using Framer and ProtoPie, enabling operators to reduce equipment downtime by 28% through proactive alert systems and explainable AI visualizations.\n Led cross-functional design sprints with 18 product managers and developers, delivering responsive UI prototypes in Figma that decreased stakeholder approval cycles by 50% and accelerated feature deployment by 6 weeks .\n Education\n Arizona State University\n Master of Science in Human-Computer Interaction (UX) \u00b7 Tempe, AZ \u00b7 GPA 4.0\n 2024 \u2013 2026\n Presidency University\n Bachelor\u2019s degree in Computer Science & Engineering \u00b7 Bangalore, IN \u00b7 CGPA 8.0 / 10\n\n## CASE STUDY: active-zen (page: work/active-zen/)\n\u2190 Work / Active Zen Open the prototype \u2197 \n The idea \n Brand \n Mandala system \n Colour \n Components \n Micro-interactions \n The flow \n Active Zen \u00b7 Wellness app \u00b7 Brand and interaction design \n Meditation for people who cannot sit still. \n Active Zen syncs breath with movement, so a run, a swim or a clean-up becomes the practice. This page is about how the brand got there and how the system moves.\n Role Brand, UI and\ninteraction design \n Focus Visual identity\nSystem, micro-interactions \n Platform iOS, dark theme \n Prototype Figma, clickable \n 01 \u00b7 The idea Stillness is hard. Movement is easy. \n Problem Sitting meditation fails the people who struggle with stillness, and nothing in the category fits a busy, moving day.\n Solution Active meditation: breathe in sync with a run, a swim or a clean-up, guided by one calm, colour-coded app.\n 02 \u00b7 Brand Start from the lotus, end with a runner inside it. \n Meditation imagery already speaks in chakras and mandalas. Active Zen keeps that language and adds the one thing it lacked: a body in motion.\n 01 Borrow the symbol Chakras and celestial patterns stand for balance, energy and mindfulness.\n 02 Build one shape A petal ring and two inner rings, drawn once and reused everywhere.\n 03 Put a body inside A flat, faceless figure per activity, so anyone can see themselves in it.\n 04 Give each a colour One hue per activity on a dark base, so the app is sorted by colour first.\n 03 \u00b7 Mandala system One mark, drawn ten ways. \n 01 Petals The lotus outline. It stays constant, so every activity reads as one family.\n 02 Rings Two concentric rings frame the figure and become the breathing timer later.\n 03 Figure The only part that changes: runner, swimmer, cyclist, lifter, dancer.\n Running Swimming Cycling Workout Cleaning Cooking Dancing Photography Gardening Sitting \n 04 \u00b7 Colour The hue follows you from tile to timer. \n Each activity owns a colour. It tints the icon, the info screen and the weekly chart, so a glance tells you what you did and when.\n Art \n Gardening \n Dance \n Photography \n Cycling \n Workout \n 05 \u00b7 Components Small parts, built to repeat. \n Every activity is assembled from the same few pieces, straight from the design file.\n Icon ring\n Two sizes per activity: swim, cycle, clean, art.\n Breath ring\n The petals fill as the session moves on. Four states, one colour per activity.\n Ring in motion\n Eight frames turn the ring while you breathe.\n Instruction cards\n A doodle and one line per step, from stand tall to relax.\n 06 \u00b7 Micro-interactions The timer is the breath. \n Setup Duration, voice, vibration, music \n Session Breath ring and cue \n Done Time, breath sync, focus Breath ring The mandala as a timer The same rings from the logo run the session, with an inhale-and-exhale cue inside.\n Haptics Vibration on breath cues Eyes stay on the road. The phone taps the rhythm instead.\n Voice and sound Optional guidance Calming spoken steps, plus a music pick.\n Wearable Connect a device Heart rate can join the session.\n 07 \u00b7 The flow Five screens, one breath. \n Home Today and the week \n Explore Pick a mandala \n Activity Spaces and benefits \n Session Breathe with it \n End How it went\n\n## CASE STUDY: aro (page: work/aro/)\n\u2190 Work / Aro Code on GitHub \u2197 \n Watch \n One glance \n Why it explains \n Design decisions \n Shipped \n Aro \u00b7 Personalized food intelligence \u00b7 VillageHacks 2026 \n Is this food right for you, right now? \n Search, scan or speak a product and get Safe, Caution or Avoid in one glance, with the reason in plain English. Built in 24 hours. Top 3 in the Pitch a Startup track, out of 600+ participants.\n Role Product design\nUX, system logic, pitch \n Built in 24 hours\nVillageHacks 2026 \n Team Founding team \n Outcome Top 3 Finalist\nPitch a Startup \n Watch on YouTube \u2197 Code on GitHub \u2197 \n 01 \u00b7 Watch The whole product, in one demo. \n 02 \u00b7 One glance A verdict before the shopper moves, a reason before they doubt it. \n In an aisle you have seconds. The result screen leads with one number and one word, then the reasons, then better options. Nothing needs reading to decide.\n Avoid Avoid This, 22 A peanut allergy is a hard stop, whatever the rest of the label says. \n Caution Caution, 54 Oats against a gluten sensitivity, sugar against a weight-loss goal. \n Safe Good Choice, 88 Nothing to flag, so the screen has nothing to say. \n Search Type a product or an ingredient.\n Scan Barcode, or point at the label.\n Speak Say the product name.\n Decide Safe, Caution or Avoid, with alternatives.\n 03 \u00b7 Why it explains AI hears and reads. It never decides. \n Speech and label photos are turned into text by AI. Everything after that is a deterministic path through a food knowledge graph and the person\u2019s own memory. So every verdict can be traced, and the explanation is the trace.\n The engine and the screen share one vocabulary\n Engine emits Screen says Verdict \n ALLERGEN_IS:Peanut Peanuts is Peanut Avoid \n SENSITIVITY:Gluten:Oats Oats may trigger Gluten sensitivity Caution \n HEALTH_GOAL:Weight loss:Sugar Sugar is high in calories/sugar which may hinder weight loss Caution \n (no codes) No allergens, diet conflicts, or disliked ingredients were detected Safe \n 04 \u00b7 Design decisions Four decisions that make a rules engine feel human. \n The engine is strict. The screen is where that strictness has to feel clear, personal and kind.\n 01 One word, one colour, one number\n The top of the result is a ring, a score and a verdict word: Avoid This in coral red, Caution in a softer coral, Good Choice in teal. The colour is readable before the word is, and the word is there for anyone who does not rely on colour.\n Teal is the only calm colour on the screen, so a good result feels like relief.\n 02 Your own rules, in the engine\u2019s own words\n A short sentence says what the verdict means. Below it, How it fits you checks your allergies, diet, sensitivities and goals one by one, with a teal tick or a coral flag. Each flag is the sentence the engine produced, so the screen never claims more than the engine found.\n Sugar as the first ingredient weighs more than sugar as the eighth, so a flag is never louder than the food deserves.\n 03 The list decides too\n Recent scans do not show codes or categories. They say Not recommended, Check ingredients or Great match, next to the score. You can re-decide on a product from the list without opening it.\n Same three outcomes everywhere, so they are learned once.\n 04 A way out, every time\n Every verdict ends in somewhere to go. Better alternatives come with their own scores, teal from 70 up, and Go with this records the choice. An Avoid never leaves you stuck in the aisle with a bad answer.\n Choices feed a health score, so the app learns what you actually pick.\n 05 \u00b7 Shipped A working app, not a prototype. \n Aro is a progressive web app with a real backend and a seeded food graph. It placed in the top three of the Pitch a Startup track at VillageHacks 2026.\n Top 3\n Pitch a Startup track, VillageHacks 2026\n 600+\n Participants at the event\n 24h\n From idea to a deployed app\n 100+\n Products in the food graph\n Front\n Next.js, TypeScript, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Framer Motion. Tesseract.js, Web Speech and BarcodeDetector for input.\n Back\n NestJS, Prisma, PostgreSQL with trigram matching, deployed on AWS with CDK.\n Watch on YouTube \u2197 \u00a0 Code on GitHub \u2197\n\n## CASE STUDY: homie (page: work/homie/)\n\u2190 Work / Homie.AI Try the prototype \u2197 \n 1-minute story \n Problem \n Research \n Ideation \n Guardrails \n The product \n Results \n The IA board \n What is next \n Homie.AI \u00b7 A screen-aware AI coach \n A coach that helps in the moment, and knows when to stay quiet. \n Homie sits on top of the software you are learning, points at what to do, then steps back on purpose until you can do it alone. The hard part was not the help. It was the rules for when to give it.\n Role Product Designer, UX Researcher, Human-AI Interaction Designer \n Timeline Spring 2026\n8-week design sprint \n Team 5 people\nGuardrail IA by our IA lead \n Tools Figma, FigJam, Claude, ElevenLabs, Figma Make \n Try the prototype \u2197 The guardrails \u2193 \n 01 \u00b7 1-minute story Ask, see, act, confirm. \n Wake the orb Choose what to share Ask for help Show me Progress Another way All steps done History \n 02 \u00b7 Problem Help lives outside the work, so people leave to find it. \n Every learner we watched left the tool at least once. The answer was always somewhere else: a video, a doc, a forum, a chat.\n Before After \n 100%\n of observed learners left the tool to find help\n 4\u20138 min\n lost to each context switch\n 50%\n of novices failed the tutorial task\n 18\u201331 min\n for tutorials that should take under 6\n Where the confusion comes from\n Four places. Tap one.\n All Tutorial User Software Interaction \n Learners translate every tutorial step into the real screen in front of them. That is where it breaks.\n 03 \u00b7 Research Capable people, blocked by fragmented support. \n Eight interviews across Figma, Unity, After Effects and other complex tools, plus a literature review, questionnaires and observation.\n Who we designed for\n Maya The overwhelmed learner Early-career designer in Figma or After Effects. Needs calm, step-by-step support that builds confidence.\n Alex The efficiency driver Self-taught developer who hates friction. Needs precise, minimal guidance.\n Sofia The creative transitioner Moving from beginner tools to professional software. Needs light guidance that protects her flow.\n Four themes became product decisions\n Sorted from raw interview notes on the affinity map.\n Key finding What changed \n The target user was not helpless. They were capable and blocked by fragmented support. From answering the user\u2019s question to guiding them inside the task. \n 04 \u00b7 Ideation One orb, one task at a time. \n Five assistant models explored: overlay, sidebar, cursor-following, voice and visual cues. The floating orb won because it is visible without becoming another distraction.\n Two risky flows, tested first\n Can users ask for help without leaving the work? Can a screen-aware AI feel visible and under their control?\n 10\n states explored: 6 paper flows, 4 AI presence states\n 12\n task checks: 6 for guidance, 6 for trust and control\n 4\n refinements after testing\n 1\n direction: a guided learning layer, not a chatbot\n 05 \u00b7 Guardrails A coach that knows when to stay quiet. \n An AI that watches your screen can help too much, too early, or in the wrong place. Our information-architecture lead wrote the rules that stop it. Five roles, one floor, a fade-out, an honest ladder and a way to ask when unsure.\n 1 \u00b7 Five roles, one floor\n Only one speaks at a time. Tap a role.\n If two want the floor: Repair \u203a Guide \u203a Explainer \n 2 \u00b7 Who speaks right now\n The orb asks five questions in order. The first yes wins. Pick a moment.\n 01 Paused, or in a restricted app? Stay silent \n 02 Error on screen, or the user looks lost? Repair speaks \n 03 Did the user ask something? Explainer answers \n 04 Same step three times, or three hints? Support steps down a stage \n 05 Next step needed, or idle over 15 seconds? Guide speaks \n 3 \u00b7 Support fades on purpose\n Across about ten operations, help steps back. Drag through a module.\n User can shift one stage either way Repair forces S1 while fixing Three hints on one step: back one stage \n 4 \u00b7 An honest pointing ladder\n Finding the right button on screen is unreliable. So the cursor only flies when Homie is sure. Slide the confidence.\n High Medium Low \n Create component\n Layers\n Properties\n Toolbar \u203a Create component\n 5 \u00b7 When it cannot tell, it asks\n Repair never guesses at a fault it cannot see. Step through.\n Back Next \n 6 \u00b7 What it admits it cannot do\n Limit How the design answers it \n Finding buttons on pro software is under about 50% accurate The confidence ladder switches off the risky options instead of pointing wrongly \n 3D viewports are hard to read from a screenshot Falls back to a region or a text path \n Confusion is only inferred from dwell time, errors and drift Elicitation Fallback: it asks the user \n A human tutor is still faster Named openly. Homie supports practice between lessons \n No voice yet Voice is a future step \n 06 \u00b7 The product Four moments, one layer. \n The prototype brings the guardrails to life inside the workspace. Orb, chat, progress and screen controls.\n 01 Orb 02 Show me 03 Another way 04 Presence \n Try the prototype \u2197 \n 07 \u00b7 Results Every round changed the product. \n 3/3\n paper testers completed the guided flow\n 3/3\n preferred guidance inside the workflow\n 2/2\n understood Pause versus Stop\n 1/1\n walkthrough confirmed the gap: where to click, not just what to do\n Learned So we \n Chat felt natural, steps cut confusion Chat overlay became a guided task panel \n The WATCHING badge was clear Presence became a transparency and trust system \n Users needed to know where to click Show Me moved from optional to required \n 08 \u00b7 The IA board The whole system, on one board. \n Eleven pages of behaviour, built by our IA lead. Tap a page to see what it settles.\n Open the FigJam board \u2197 \n 09 \u00b7 What is next From prototype to product. \n A five-person founding team has started R&D across screen understanding, visual guidance, conversational support and practice-based learning.\n Status\n R&D started, team formed\n Next host app: Figma in the browser\n Next feature: the animated Show Me cursor as the top rung of the ladder\n Looking for\n Developers\n Design partners\n Early investors\n\n## CASE STUDY: keep (page: work/keep/)\n\u2190 Work / Keep Open the product \u2197 \n Overview \n Who it is for \n Solution \n Design decisions \n All screens \n Feedback \n Places \n Re-live \n Limits and next \n Now \n Keep \u00b7 A smart layer for your photos, built with Meta AI \n Drop it in once. Ask for it later. \n Think Google Photos or Apple Photos, made smart and interactive. Share what happened this past week, and later ask for it in your own words. Keep shows you the original, not a guess.\n Role Product Designer\nConcept and design \n Timeline October 2026 \n Team Duo\nBuilt with a teammate \n Status Shipped, and in use \n Open the product \u2197 Code on GitHub \u2197 \n 01 \u00b7 Overview Your memories live in five apps, and none of them can answer a question. \n The photo is in the camera roll. The name is in Notes. The voice memo is somewhere else. When someone asks \u201cwhat was his name, where does he work?\u201d, you search four places and give up.\n Today With Keep \n Concept Your camera roll, with Meta AI on top.\n Shipped A working app you install on your phone.\n In use We use it ourselves, to see where it goes.\n 02 \u00b7 Who it is for The Designated Rememberer, the unofficial memory of their circle. \n Everyone asks this person. \u201cWhat was that restaurant?\u201d \u201cWhat did Priya say she wanted?\u201d \u201cWhen did we last see Jake?\u201d\n How to spot them\n They take the group photos and are never in them\n They text themselves details like \u201cMina hates cilantro\u201d\n They already use five apps for this, and none is searchable by person\n They dread the day it fails them\n Who it is not for\n People who live fully in the moment\n Minimalists who rely on their photos\n Privacy-first people, since Keep stores details about others\n Anyone nobody ever asks\n Everyone asks the rememberer. Keep remembers for the rememberer. \n 03 \u00b7 Solution Keep, revisit, ask, and stand where it happened. \n Tap a screen.\n Keep Photo, video, voice, note, quote, person, place or file. One tap each.\n Revisit A calendar of orbs. Every day you kept something shows as a photo.\n Ask \u201cWho is Jake?\u201d The answer comes with the original memory.\n Re-live Point the camera at a place and meet the person who was there.\n 04 \u00b7 Design decisions Four decisions that shaped everything else. \n 01 The calendar is the memory\n Not a feed, not folders. Each day is one orb with the photo you kept. You find things by remembering when , which is how people actually remember.\n A soft gradient shifts with the season, so a month feels like a month.\n 02 You push. Keep never nags.\n No streaks, no prompts, no \u201cadd something today\u201d. Look-backs only point at the past, and arrive as a notification, never as a badge on the home screen.\n Keep is for the person who fears losing small things, not one who fears falling behind.\n 03 Answers show the original\n Ask in your own words. Keep answers from what you kept and shows the quote, the photo, the voice note itself. When nothing matches, it says so.\n An answer you cannot check is worse than no answer.\n 04 Voice is a button you hold\n Hold the black mic and talk, release to stop. Same as a voice message you already send. The orb breathes and a live waveform follows your voice.\n Nothing on the screen explains it, so the gesture has to feel obvious.\n 05 \u00b7 All screens Every screen, in one place. \n Tap any screen to enlarge.\n Calendar Every kept day is an orb \n A day Everything from one day \n A person Who she is, kept once \n Look back A year ago, one swipe at a time \n Keep a photo Who is in it, and how they stood \n Keep a note Words, exactly as said \n Keep a person Where you met, what to remember \n Keep a voice Hold to talk \n Ask Answers with the original \n Places Everything you kept, on a map \n Re-live The person, back where they were \n Year Zoom out to the year \n First run Starts empty \n Dark Light first, dark in Profile \n 06 \u00b7 Feedback What I got wrong, and what changed. \n Early feedback said the payoff comes too late, a photo alone misses who was there, and the audience was too wide. We are also learning from using it ourselves.\n Feedback Decision \n A photo alone misses who was there People are the first structure. Tag who is in a photo, and ask \u201cWho is Jake?\u201d Built \n The audience is too broad Narrowed to the Designated Rememberer Decided \n Memory pays off too late to build a habit Start from the past: share the last week or so on day one, so look-backs and answers work straight away Next \n Capture on Meta glasses Left out. It breaks \u201cyou push\u201d, and the privacy risk is real. A later capture surface at most Not now \n 07 \u00b7 Places Everywhere you have kept something, as a map of your life. \n Photos taken in Keep remember where they were. Open Places and your history of places shows up as photos on a map.\n Tap a place Open the day you were there, see the person who was with you, or get directions.\n Stacks, not clutter Photos from the same spot group together, with a count.\n Near me The locate button shows what you kept within 200 m of where you stand.\n Feeds Re-live The same location narrows which memories can come back to life in a room.\n 08 \u00b7 Re-live The person who isn\u2019t there any more, back in the room. \n This is where the Meta collaboration comes to life. Tag a photo with who is in it and where they stood. Later, point your camera at the same spot and a 3D avatar of that person appears there, with a glass card of everything you kept about that moment.\n Honest about how it works\n Limit Why it is fine for now \n The avatar is generated from the name It stands for the person, it does not claim to be them \n Pose and spot are tagged by you Nothing is guessed about people \n Matching needs a similar view of the same place With location on, only memories within 200 m are candidates \n 09 \u00b7 Limits and next What it does not do yet, and what comes next. \n Limits today\n Memories live on the device. Back up and restore, no sync yet\n Voice notes and videos keep a still and a duration\n Search is keyword matching\n Next\n Share your recent past on day one, from the camera roll\n Meaning-based search\n Sync across devices\n Capture beyond the phone, with the privacy risk named first\n 10 \u00b7 Now Still being built, and in use. \n We are building Keep in the open and using it ourselves to learn what to improve next. It is in active scaling, and this case study will be updated as that goes.\n Open the product \u2197 \u00a0 Code on GitHub \u2197\n\n## CASE STUDY: livesync (page: work/livesync/)\n\u2190 Work / LiveSync \n The trigger \n The gap \n The flow \n Four features \n What next \n LiveSync \u00b7 Google Photos concept \u00b7 June 2025 \n Photos that share themselves. \n A small change inside Google Photos: shared albums that fill up in real time while a trip is still happening. No new app, no links to chase.\n Role UX research\nProduct design \n Approach Micro-restructuring \n Platform Google Photos,\niOS and Android \n Type Concept study \n 01 \u00b7 The trigger One road trip, four phones, a thousand photos. \n LA to San Diego with three friends. Everyone shot something. Nobody had the full trip. Sharing meant waiting, asking and chasing links.\n Today \u201cHey, can you send me those pics later?\u201d \n \u2192 With LiveSync \u201cThey\u2019re already in the album.\u201d \n 02 \u00b7 The gap Nobody wants another photo app. \n Apps that sync albums exist, but each asks people to leave the place their photos already live. The opening is real-time sharing inside Google Photos, which has 2B+ users, runs on iOS and Android and already has shared albums.\n Cluster Syncs only while the app is open\n Kululu Joins through a QR code\n FamilyAlbum Another app to install\n LiveSync Inside the app people already use\n 03 \u00b7 The flow Four taps from \u201c+\u201d to a live album. \n The feature hides in plain sight: one new row in the create menu, then the album screen people already know.\n Tap + From the Photos tab \n Create new Shared album (Live Sync) \n Set up Title, toggle, invite \n Collections Shared albums, live \n 04 \u00b7 Four features Small additions, one shift in behaviour. \n 01 Create from \u201c+\u201d Invite, accept, done. Photos sync as you shoot.\n 02 One-tap pause A persistent \u201cLiveSync On\u201d notification you can pause.\n 03 Social layer \u201cKaran just added 3 photos\u201d and quick reactions.\n 04 Privacy controls Wi-Fi only, location zones and optional approval.\n 05 \u00b7 What next Evolve the product, do not reinvent it. \n The best ideas amplify what people already do and fix what everyone accepts as \u201cjust how it is.\u201d\n Auto-pause after a set time Location-based start Face-match sync Smart Wi-Fi sync Back-sync on request\n\n## CASE STUDY: mayo (page: work/mayo/)\n\u2190 Work / Mayo Clinic Live prototype \u2197 \n \u2190 All projects Live prototype \u2197 \n 30-second story \n The problem \n Research \n Co-design \n Synthesis \n Service blueprint \n Ideation & flow \n V1 & testing \n What I changed \n Confidence layer \n Depth on demand \n AI interaction model \n Design decisions \n The screens \n Accessibility \n Validation \n Reflection \n Mayo Clinic \u00b7 AI-native clinical product \u00b7 Smart Hematology Triage \n Nurses weren't short on judgment. They were short on time to use it. \n Smart Hematology Triage is an AI-assisted workspace that brings fragmented patient data into one view. The AI recommends and explains; the clinician decides. I drove the design end to end, from research with Mayo clinicians to validated prototype.\n Role CX design \u00b7 UX research \u00b7 human-AI interaction \n Timeline 16 weeks \u00b7 Fall 2025 \n Team 5-person team, with an AI researcher and ML developer \n Partners Mayo Clinic hematologists, triage nurses, subject-matter expert \n Open the live prototype \u2197 \u2190 All projects \n The triage decision workspace: the four-step decision flow with the AI suggestion, beside the clinical triage guide. \n If you only read one thing The 30-second story\n Project summary + outcome metrics \n 30 seconds \n 15 min full read \u2193 \n The problem Hematology triage nurses spent most of each referral assembling context across 3\u20135 systems , not making the clinical decision.\n What I did Designed one triage workspace where an AI recommends with explainable confidence and the nurse verifies and decides in the chart they already trust.\n What changed Triage became about twice as fast in testing, urgent cases surfaced sooner, and clinicians rated it easy to use.\n ~50 % Faster triage Before vs after in testing, from about 30 to 15 minutes per referral.\n 40 % Less search time Less time hunting for patient information across systems.\n 3 \u00d7 Faster to surface urgent cases High-risk referrals identified sooner.\n 80 SUS score In the validation round, above the 68 industry benchmark.\n AI decision flow \n How every referral moves: AI recommends, the nurse decides, the model learns. \n AI Aggregates Pulls 3\u20135 fragmented systems into one view of the patient.\n AI Recommends Predicted outcome, priority and confidence, with the signals behind it.\n Authority passes to the nurse \n Nurse Verifies Checks the reasoning in the clinical chart she already uses.\n Nurse Decides \n Accept or adjust \u2192 4-step triage action Override \u2192 logged with the decision \n System Learns Overrides and outcomes flow back so the model sharpens over time.\n Guardrails No auto-approve, ever Low confidence \u2192 human review Every action logged \n 01 \u00b7 The problem Time was the bottleneck, not the decision. \n Fragmentation model \n The U.S. loses an estimated $265 billion a year to administrative complexity alone (Shrank et al., JAMA, 2019). In hematology triage, where a delay can postpone a cancer diagnosis by weeks, nurses lose their most valuable minutes before clinical judgment even begins.\n The information existed. It was spread across 3\u20135 systems, so every referral started with a search. Switch views to see the change I was designing for.\n One triage workspace \n EHR \n Lab results \n Imaging \n Clinical notes \n Referral documents \n Triage nurse \n Before After \n 3\u20135 \n systems opened per referral. The nurse is the integration layer, rebuilding context by hand every time.\n 20\u201330 % Of the shift spent searching Not deciding.\n ~45 % Of referrals arrive incomplete Missing information to chase.\n 12\u201318 min Lost per referral To data-hunting alone.\n 50\u201360 %+ Burnout linked to workload The cost lands on the people.\n \"A lot of my time goes into piecing together charts instead of making the actual decision.\" Attending hematologist, stakeholder interview \n 02 \u00b7 Research Breadth from a survey, depth from the floor. \n Research triangulation \n I ran the research as methodological triangulation: a survey for scale, in-person contextual inquiry for depth, plus workflow observation and the literature. No single method could tell us both how common a problem was and why it happened.\n 52 Nurse survey Scale: how widespread each problem is\n 6 Contextual inquiry sessions In person, with physicians and triage nurses\n Observe Workflow observation Think-aloud on real referrals\n Lit Literature Cost, burnout and diagnostic thresholds\n 3 Personas Purposive sampling across age and digital comfort\n Survey findings \n What the numbers said.\n Select a finding to read the voice behind it.\n 100% said the AI must support, not approve, clinical decisions. That single finding set the boundary for the whole product: the AI could assist, surface and explain, but the human would always hold the decision.\n Personas \n Three roles, one shared frustration.\n I sampled from Gen-Z nurses to nurses over 50 who avoid digital tools, because adoption lives or dies on the least tech-comfortable user.\n Hematology Triage Nurse\n \"I spend most of my time finding labs and notes before I can even start thinking through the case.\"\n Needs: organized information that speeds up clinical reasoning Handles first-pass triage and decides urgency under time pressure.\n Attending Hematologist\n \"A lot of my time goes into piecing together charts instead of making the actual decision.\"\n Needs: consolidated context with less overhead Reviews complex referrals between clinic responsibilities.\n Triage Coordinator\n \"The information is in the EHR, but I have to jump across tabs and reports to understand what's going on.\"\n Needs: unified visibility across sources Tracks referral status, labs and follow-ups across patients.\n 03 \u00b7 Co-design The AI's logic came from the clinicians. \n Clinician working sessions \n I didn't want the AI to reason in a way nurses had never seen. So we mapped the real triage pathways live with Mayo hematologists, and those decision trees became the logic the model and the interface share. Select a photo to enlarge it.\n Neutropenia pathway , mapped live with a Mayo hematologist. \n Thrombocytosis pathway , co-designed the same way. \n From whiteboard to product \n The same pathways, inside the product. \n The decision trees we mapped with clinicians became the Hematology Triage Decision Algorithm in the product. When the AI recommends a path, the nurse can see it on the same protocol she already follows.\n 04 \u00b7 Synthesis Everything pointed to three challenges. \n Affinity synthesis \u2192 challenges \n I affinity-mapped the survey, interviews and observations together. Three clusters rose to the top, and each became a design challenge.\n Challenge 01 Time is the bottleneck Bring key patient context into one place and organize it around the decision.\n Challenge 02 A system, not a tool Triage depends on labs, notes, timelines and handoffs. Connect them.\n Challenge 03 Accountable AI Assist and explain, with visible data and easy control. The clinician decides.\n Not How do we add AI to triage?\n \u2192 \n How might we save clinical time by designing a triage system that is fast, trusted, and easy to use? \n 05 \u00b7 Service blueprint A journey shows the screen. A blueprint shows the system. \n Service blueprint \n A journey map shows what the nurse sees. The blueprint also shows the back stage: the systems, the AI model and the team handoffs that make each moment possible. Select a stage to follow it top to bottom.\n 1 \u00b7 Referral arrives \n 2 \u00b7 Open & review \n 3 \u00b7 AI recommendation \n 4 \u00b7 Verify & decide \n 5 \u00b7 Handoff \n Nurse actions \n Sees a prioritized queue\n Opens the case and scans the unified overview\n Reads the AI recommendation and confidence\n Checks the chart, then accepts or adjusts\n Submits and documents\n Front-stage UI \n Referral queue with priority and status\n Unified overview: labs, EHR and timeline in one\n Decision flow: outcome, confidence, signals\n Familiar chart and a scaffolded triage guide\n Confirmation and team messaging\n Line of visibility \n \u2191 Recommendation + confidence AI \u2193 Accept / override logged \n Back-stage AI \n Intake, routing and prioritization\n Aggregates 3\u20135 systems into one record\n Triage model: prediction, confidence, signals\n Logs acceptance or override\n Writes to the record and notifies the team\n Support \n Model trained on the same clinical charts nurses already use : one shared representation across training, output and UI. AI developer + Mayo engineer \u00b7 physician stakeholders \u00b7 guardrail: AI never auto-approves a triage decision \n The AI surfaces in exactly one place, stage 3, where it crosses the line of visibility. The anchor decision sits in the support lane: the model reasons over the same charts nurses reason with, and that representation is what appears on screen.\n 06 \u00b7 Ideation & flow I sketched fast, then let clinicians push back. \n Ideation board \n Early concepts centred on AI summaries, risk flags and visual timelines. I sketched quickly, pressure-tested ideas with clinicians through affinity and concept mapping, then shaped them into one referral-to-decision flow. Drag to explore the raw board.\n Clinician feedback that shaped v1 \n The AI must not read \n\n## CASE STUDY: pmip (page: work/pmip/)\n\u2190 Work / Siemens PMIP Live prototype \u2197 \n \u2190 All projects \n 30-second story \n Context \n The problem \n Research \n Insights \n Flow & structure \n Speed vs depth \n Patterns over time \n Decision log \n Human + AI \n The system \n The screens \n Testing \n Shipping \n Results \n Siemens \u00b7 Predictive Maintenance Intelligence Platform \n The model could predict failures. Engineers still weren't acting. \n PMIP already had working machine-learning models. What it lacked was an experience that let experienced industrial engineers understand a prediction, check it against their own judgment, and act on it. That was my job.\n Role Product Designer \n Timeline Apr 2022 \u2013 Jul 2024 \n Scope Research synthesis to implementation QA \n Worked with ML, data science, product, frontend, industrial engineers, QA \n Operational overview, rebuilt from the project documentation. Asset data other than Motor M-204 is illustrative. \n If you only read one thing The 30-second story\n Project summary + outcome metrics Documented \n 30 seconds 15 min full read \u2193 \n The problem PMIP's models could predict equipment failures, but engineers didn't trust or act on a conclusion with no reasoning. \n What I did Redesigned the experience around the engineer's decision: summary, evidence and AI reasoning on request , ending in a human decision.\n What changed Engineers could understand, check and act on predictions, and every decision fed back to the ML team.\n 28 % Less equipment downtime The models made the predictions. The experience helped operators act on them earlier.\n 35 % Better task completion On complex energy-management tasks, after the research-driven redesign.\n 50 % Shorter approval cycles Interactive prototypes and structured design sprints replaced slow reviews.\n 6 wk Faster feature deployment Earlier alignment let engineering start sooner.\n AI decision flow Doc + synthesis \n How every prediction moves: AI informs, people decide, the system learns. \n Model Detects Finds an anomaly in sensor data and predicts a failure.\n Model Explains Summary, evidence, AI reasoning. Confidence always with its evidence and precedent.\n Authority passes to the engineer \n Engineer Evaluates Checks the evidence against their own experience.\n Engineer Decides \n Agree \u2192 maintenance, context carried over Disagree \u2192 reason \u2192 monitor or escalate \n System Learns Outcomes and disagreements reach the product and ML teams.\n Guarantees The AI is never the final decision-maker No confidence without evidence Every disagreement is captured with a reason \n 01 \u00b7 Context Maintenance was either too late or too early. \n Maintenance models comparison Documented \n Siemens plants run motors, pumps, turbines, compressors and heat exchangers. Traditionally, they were maintained in one of two ways, and both cost money: waiting for a failure means unplanned downtime, and replacing parts on a calendar means replacing healthy parts.\n PMIP was built to move maintenance to a third model, prediction. The intelligence already existed. The step that didn't work yet was the one in the middle: an operator validating the prediction . That is where my work sat.\n Model 1 Reactive\n Equipment fails\n Production is affected\n Team investigates\n Repair happens\n Cost: unplanned downtime.\n Model 2 Scheduled preventive\n A fixed interval is reached\n Inspection or replacement happens\n Whether the part needs it or not\n Cost: healthy parts replaced early, crews sent unnecessarily.\n Model 3 \u00b7 PMIP Predictive\n Equipment sensors\n Operational data\n Anomaly detection\n Failure prediction\n Operator validation \u00b7 my focus\n Preventive maintenance\n Goal: fix what is about to fail, before it fails.\n 02 \u00b7 The problem The alert gave a conclusion. Engineers needed the reasoning. \n Question wall \u00b7 old alert vs PMIP Documented \n When I joined, the models were working and generating predictions, but operators weren't consistently trusting or acting on them. An alert looked like the card below. For the machine, that's an output. For an engineer, it's where the investigation starts.\n Switch to PMIP to see which of the engineer's nine questions the redesign answers, and where.\n ALERT Motor M-204 Bearing failure predicted HIGH RISK \n Old alert \n PMIP \n 0 of 9 questions answered\n Why? Level 3 \u00b7 AI reasoning \n Which sensor changed? Level 2 \u00b7 Evidence \n How abnormal is it? Level 2 \u00b7 12.4 vs 2\u20138 mm/s \n How long has it been abnormal? Level 2 \u00b7 72 hours \n Sudden spike or gradual? Equipment health timeline \n How confident is the model? Level 1 + Level 3 \u00b7 87% \n Which signals contributed most? Level 3 \u00b7 signal influence \n Has this happened before? Level 3 \u00b7 24 of 28 failed \n What should I do? Level 1 \u00b7 Inspect bearing assembly \n Problem reframe Documented \n I stopped asking how to display predictions.\n The model might be accurate, but if an experienced engineer doesn't trust it enough to act, its value is limited. I called this the trust problem, and it changed the question the team was answering.\n From How do we display AI predictions?\n \u2192 \n To How might we help experienced industrial operators understand the severity, evidence, reasoning and appropriate action behind an AI-generated prediction, without replacing their professional judgment? \n 03 \u00b7 Research We watched the work. Not just the interviews. \n Research funnel Documented \n Industrial workflows are highly contextual, so I didn't want to redesign from dashboard conventions. Across the broader Siemens research program, we ran and synthesized 85+ contextual inquiry sessions , watching engineers, technicians and operators respond to real alerts.\n 85+ Contextual inquiry sessions Industrial engineers, technicians and operators\n 60\u201390 Minute observations Debriefs and structured notes\n FigJam Affinity mapping Patterns, not individual comments\n 3 User types Personas and journey maps\n 6 Design principles From five key insights\n Said vs observed workflow Documented \n What people said and what they did were different.\n What engineers said I review the alert and decide. \n What we observed \n Alert Equipment history Vibration trend Temperature Previous maintenance Alert again Decision \n Every decision hid an investigation across several places. My job was to bring that evidence to the alert itself.\n User types Documented Depth meter is my reading \n \"Industrial operator\" wasn't one person.\n Senior Maintenance Engineer\n \"Show me the evidence so I can determine whether I trust this prediction.\"\n \u00a0Deep\n Often 15+ years with the equipment. Remembers past failures, and because of that expertise, more skeptical of unexplained AI.\n Energy Management Operator\n \"What needs my attention right now?\"\n \u00a0Scan\n Monitors many assets at once. Needs rapid scanning, prioritization and situational awareness.\n Maintenance Technician\n Which equipment? What's wrong? What's the priority? What do I do?\n \u00a0Execute\n Enters close to execution and needs clarity on the inspection or maintenance required.\n Synthesis chain Documented \n From observations to product opportunities.\n I didn't treat every comment as a requirement. I looked for recurring behaviour and carried it through this chain, one step at a time.\n Observations Patterns Personas Journey maps Pain points Design principles Product opportunities \n 04 \u00b7 Insights Five insights became six principles. \n Insight \u2192 principle \u2192 screen map Doc + synthesis \n The insights and principles come straight from the research synthesis. The links between them are how I applied them. Select an insight to trace it to the principles it shaped and where it shows up in the product.\n Design goal Create an operational decision-support experience that transforms complex predictive-model outputs into prioritized, explainable, actionable maintenance decisions while preserving expert human judgment.\n Insight \n 01 Accuracy and trust are different problems Make the evidence interpretable, not the AI look more confident. \n 02 Experts didn't need less information The problem was poorly structured information. \n 03 Engineers think in patterns over time A single abnormal value isn't enough. \n 04 Every alert needs an action \"High risk\" describes a state, not a next step. \n 05 AI should support human judgment AI detects and explains. The human evaluates and decides. \n Principle \n P1 Show the why behind every AI recommendation\n P2 Minimize cognitive load during active incident response\n P3 Surface critical information without unnecessary navigation\n P4 Build trust through transparency, not authority\n P5 Design for expert users, don't oversimplify the domain\n P6 Give every alert a clear next action\n Shows up in \n Ranked dashboard\n Three-level alert\n Evidence & AI reasoning\n Equipment health timeline\n Human decision\n Maintenance action\n 05 \u00b7 Flow & structure I designed around the decision journey, not the backend. \n User flow Documented Step-to-screen pairing is mine \n Once I understood the operator's mental model, the flow became Monitor \u2192 Prioritize \u2192 Investigate \u2192 Explain \u2192 Validate \u2192 Decide \u2192 Act \u2192 Track , and every major screen answers\n\n## CASE STUDY: promove (page: work/promove/)\n\u2190 Work / Promove Watch on YouTube \u2197 \n 1-minute story \n Context \n Final product \n Learning from the pros \n Artist language \n One canvas \n The interface \n Motion \n Problems \n Thinking \n We tested it \n Research \n Reflections \n Behind the scenes \n pro-move by Procreate \u00b7 A motion capture concept \n What if your drawing could move the moment you do? \n Film yourself, and your own 2D character copies you, all inside Procreate. Plain layers and frames, or a simple rig you can polish. Built for doodle artists, not technical animators.\n Team Lakshman, Design Engineer\nKalyan, UX Designer \n Competition SVSD Design Competition\nOct 2026 \n Tools MediaPipe, WebGL2, ComfyUI \n Scope Interaction Design, Prototyping, AI Prototyping \n Watch on YouTube \u2197 The 1-minute story \u2193 \n 01 \u00b7 1-minute story Your character copies you. \n Why it matters How it works Inside Procreate Capture Filmstrip Turn and stretch Rig check Export \n 02 \u00b7 Context Artists can build the body. Motion is the hard part. \n In Procreate, an artist draws a character with full control over pose, proportion and personality. Making it move is a different job. You go frame by frame, a second of animation is twenty-four drawings, and the rigging tools that could help are built for technical animators, not doodle artists.\n And movement is personal. Everyone carries their own body language: how they lean, turn, shrug. That is exactly what redrawing by hand loses, and exactly what a camera can capture.\n 24\n drawings for one second of animation\n 33\n body joints tracked from the camera\n 0\n drawings sent to the cloud\n In Procreate today With pro-move \n Moving a character Redraw every frame Perform once, in front of the camera \n Personality Redrawn by hand each time Captured from your own body \n Rigging Bones, weights, graphs Tap a limb to turn or stretch it \n Where it lives Separate technical tools One tab inside Procreate \n How might we Our answer \n Let a doodle artist animate without learning to rig? Perform on camera. The rig is built from the drawing. \n Keep the artist's own drawing intact when it moves? Blend at the joints, and repaint only the spots that break. \n Make it feel like part of Procreate, not another app? One Rig tab in Actions, takes as layers, exports that open in Animation Assist. \n 03 \u00b7 Final product Perform, capture, refine, take it back. \n pro-move is an add-on that lives inside Procreate, as one Rig tab in the Actions menu. Everything runs on the device, so a drawing never leaves it.\n Perform\n Step in front of the camera. Your character copies your joints and your face: blinks, mouth, smile, brows, head tilt.\n Capture\n Pose: a three-second countdown, one drawing. Motion: up to ten seconds, keeping the drawings where you move most.\n Refine\n Tap a limb to turn or stretch it. Where a joint tears, a local AI repaints only that spot.\n Take it back\n PSD layers that Animation Assist plays directly. Layered SVG, sprite sheet, video and an AI pack.\n 04 \u00b7 Learning from the pros We borrowed the behaviour, not the interface. \n TVPaint's rigging tool does what we needed, but for technical animators on a desktop. We studied how it behaves and translated each idea for an artist with a finger. Its code is not public, so everything we kept is an equivalent of what we could see.\n 01 Bones and Animate mode Blue kite-shaped bones lie over the drawing. In Animate mode the bones turn blue, and you rotate a bone by clicking it. This became our blue bones and turn circle.\n 02 The timeline of keys Each rotation creates a key automatically on a stack of layers. Powerful, but a technical animator's view. We replaced it with a filmstrip of drawings.\n 03 Stretch with Ctrl The tutorial says to hold Ctrl, click the bone and drag to stretch it. There is no Ctrl key on an iPad, so this became the diamond handle.\n In TVPaint In pro-move Why \n Translucent blue bones Kite-shaped bones, wide near the joint Tap a bone to pick the part \n Rotate circle A circle handle you drag Turning feels like turning \n Ctrl-drag to stretch A diamond handle on the bone tip An iPad has no Ctrl key \n Rig and Animate modes Blue is this frame, amber is every frame The colour says how far a change reaches \n A stack of timeline layers One row of drawings A technical view, replaced by a filmstrip \n 05 \u00b7 Artist language Say it the way an artist would. \n No physics, no weights. The character is a hierarchy, and settings flow down: set the feel on an arm and everything inside follows, until you override one part. Overrides are marked, and a part's twin can mirror its edits.\n Character\n Head eyes, brows, mouth \n Torso jacket hem \n Arms upper arm, forearm, hand, five fingers \n Legs thigh, shin, shoe \n Technical term Artist wording \n Smoothness Clean-up: Sketchy to Clean \n Follow-through, bounce Feel: Snappy, Smooth, Floaty \n Raw versus smoothed graph Before and After preview \n Inverse kinematics Pose brush: drag to bend \n Motion curve A dotted arc trail \n Mesh and weights Advanced only \n 06 \u00b7 One canvas The canvas is the interface. \n The first version split one job, animating your character, across separate places: a capture bar, a sheet of drawings, a layers panel, a rig panel and a preview window. An artist had to hunt for each one.\n So we asked a different question: what if every control sat on the drawing, next to the thing it changes? Capture opens from the top left. The take lands in a filmstrip at the bottom. A small pill appears beside the part you tap. A slim strip on the right holds the feel. One rule decided what could be hidden: a control may only go if it has a visible twin somewhere else.\n We compared nine directions as grey-box boards. Pick one. \n Safe to simplify\n Where controls live\n Wording\n The arrival sheet\n Long lists of settings\n Risky to cut\n Hands-free gestures: false triggers, small rooms\n Hold-to-record: the performer stands far from the iPad\n Hold and pinch: invisible to a new user\n Never simplify\n Marked jumps\n Undo and redo\n Turn versus stretch\n Every body part, reachable\n The original take, one press away\n 07 \u00b7 The interface From a recording to a drawing you can edit. \n Show motion, do not describe it. Feel tiles are small animated waves. Path is a dotted trail where the spacing shows speed. Hold Original to see the raw camera take. Amber seams mark frames that no longer flow. Tips appear once, then get out of the way.\n A take becomes drawings\n A recording turns into an arrival sheet of drawings picked where the body moves most, with a Few to Many slider. Reordering marks the spots that break the flow and lets the artist add an in-between. TVPaint-style bones, a turn circle and stretch bring editing onto the character.\n 13 Arrival sheet After recording, a sheet shows the drawings picked where the body moves most, with a Few to Many slider and Retake, Rig it and Keep.\n 14 Layers panel and jump marks The take as a list of frames. After a reorder, orange notes mark two spots that no longer flow, each with Add in-between or Leave it. The previous frame shows as an onion skin.\n 15 Rig mode and preview window The panel becomes a colour-coded part tree with Clean-up and Feel cards. A small window at the top right loops only the selected part, Before and After.\n 16 Pose brush and part tree Tap a part on the canvas to select it in the tree, then drag to bend it on this frame.\n 17 A scene behind the character The playground illustration became a Procreate-style layer, with the capture bar shown at the bottom.\n 18 Transform tool Drag the character to move it or a corner to resize. It applies to every frame of every take.\n 19 A take inside the scene The Layers panel shows a new take and a toast, with a first-use hint on dragging frames.\n 20 TVPaint-style bones Every bone drawn as a translucent diamond. A hint tells the artist to tap a bone, then drag to turn it.\n 21 Rotate circle Dragging turns the upper arm around its joint, with a circle showing the turn. Children follow.\n 22 Stretch brush Dragging along the forearm makes it longer, with a live percentage (here 189 percent). Range is 40 to 220 percent.\n 23 Joint balls, off and on Round joints painted from the character's own colours fade in where an arm bends or stretches. Before and after, side by side.\n Every moment between taps\n We designed what happens around each action: a beating countdown that sits away from the face, a REC chip and a red canvas edge while recording, a visible landing slot while dragging, and a toast that says what moved.\n 24 Capture bar at rest Always visible: Start camera or Try demo. The scene is clear underneath.\n 25 Tooltip on Try demo A one-time callout explains that no camera is needed to try it.\n 26 Countdown A beating number and Get into your pose.\n 27 Recording A REC chip with the time, and a red capture bar showing 0:02 of 0:10.\n 28 Arrival sheet with preview Preview plays the picked drawings on the canvas. Left out drawings are tagged.\n 29 Dragging a frame The row lifts and the list opens a slot, so there is a visible landing place.\n 3\n\n## CASE STUDY: uni-verse (page: work/uni-verse/)\n\u2190 Work / Uni-Verse \n 30-second view \n The problem \n The product \n What students said \n The gap \n People \n Structure \n Design system \n Design decisions \n Tested \n Reflection \n Behind the scenes \n Uni-Verse \u00b7 Campus engagement \u00b7 ASU GIT, Fall 2025 \n Helping students find people, events, and belonging. \n A student-first campus app that puts events, communities, friends and campus movement in one live view, so showing up gets easier. Best UX/UI Design Award, ASU GIT, Fall 2025.\n Role Product design\nUX research, behavioural UX \n Timeline 8 weeks \n Team 2 designers \n Outcome Best UX/UI Design\nASU GIT, Fall 2025 \n 01 \u00b7 30-second view Three things stop students. Five ideas answer them. \n Uni-Verse turns everyday campus life into small, low-risk chances to meet people.\n Situation Hard to make friends and adapt to a new culture 72% felt lonely after arriving.\n \u2192 Solution Communities Interest-based spaces to find people, groups and shared belonging.\n Situation No real-time view of events and campus resources Events are missed because they are hard to see.\n \u2192 Solution Social Heat Map Real-time view of campus activities, events and student zones.\n Situation Transport, clashing schedules and going alone 89% want connections, yet many skip events without company.\n \u2192 Solution Tag-Along Join friends at events, so showing up feels easier and safer.\n Solution Shuttle Tracking Live shuttle updates that make getting around less stressful.\n Student-first vision: a direction built around belonging, access, participation and trust.\n 72%\n of students felt lonely after arriving\n 89%\n want new connections\n 9/10\n rated Tag-Along in testing\n 9\n features no compared platform has\n 02 \u00b7 The problem New students arrive with plenty to join and nobody to join it with. \n Freshmen and international students struggle to connect, adapt and stay engaged. Loneliness follows, and so do missed chances.\n 01 Hard to build friendships and adapt to a new culture.\n 02 No real-time view of events, communities and campus resources.\n 03 Transport, clashing schedules and the hesitation to go alone.\n The goal: a digital companion that reduces isolation, connects communities and makes taking part simple and welcoming.\n 03 \u00b7 The product Find it, join it, bring someone. \n Eight screens carry the whole loop: arrive, see what is happening, pick something, and go with people.\n Login Apple, Google, Meta or email. Recovery one tap away. \n Home Tag-Along and clubs pinned on top, a live map preview, then events. \n Social Heat Map Filter All, Tag-Alongs, Events, Shuttles on one live map. \n Search Image tiles for Fitness, Gaming, Sports, Music, Books and Arts. \n Create One entry for a Tag-Along, a cohort or an event. \n Communities A grid of groups, with Explore for the ones you can join now. \n Tag-Along Who, when, where and how many have joined, then one button. \n Profile Interests and connections, the start of a friendship. \n 04 \u00b7 What students said The loneliness was real, and the wish to fix it was louder. \n We surveyed 54 students to test what we had seen first hand. Many hesitate to attend alone, cancel without company, and struggle to find peers for study, sport or hobbies. Missed shuttles and poor event visibility came up again and again.\n Experience loneliness Often or sometimes, after arriving \n 72% \n Want new connections Desire to make new relationships \n 89% \n Need emotional support Emotional or social support from others \n 89% \n Cultural adaptation issues Found it hard to adapt to the culture \n 74% \n Difficulty making friends Found it hard to make friends \n 33% \n 05 \u00b7 The gap Three apps find the event. None help you walk in. \n Every platform we reviewed lists events. None handle the hard part: going with someone, getting there, and finding your people.\n ThriveUp\n Event discovery and networking\n Popularity 2/5 Usability 2/5 \n Strengths Focused on event discovery\n Networking approach\n Weaknesses Purpose is unclear\n Hard to navigate\n No full college database\n Opportunities Clearer value proposition\n Structured database\n Threats Established platforms\n Low retention from poor UX\n Eventee\n Mobile event app for universities\n Popularity 4/5 Usability 2/5 \n Strengths Used by top universities\n Real-time updates\n Personalised schedules\n Weaknesses Complicated UI\n Limited search\n Opportunities Simpler UI\n Central database\n Threats Better usability elsewhere\n User frustration\n Modern Campus\n Student engagement platform\n Popularity 3/5 Usability 3/5 \n Strengths Clean design\n Club management\n Event and organisation sections\n Weaknesses Events page loads slowly\n Weak college search\n No transport features\n Opportunities Faster performance\n Transport integration\n Threats More streamlined rivals\n Performance hurts retention\n Feature Uni-Verse ThriveUp Eventee Modern Campus \n Cultural adaptation \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Centralised event hub \u25cf \u25cf \u25cf \u25cf \n Last-minute event joining \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Real-time heat map \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Live event updates \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Study cohort system \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Tag-along \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Transportation integration \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Sports partner matching \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n Interactive features \u25cf \u25cb \u25cf \u25cb \n Vibe matching \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n University building finder \u2605 \u25cf \u25cb \u25cb \u25cb \n \u2605 only Uni-Verse offers it among the four. \u25cf offered, \u25cb not offered.\n 06 \u00b7 People Two students, one missing bridge. \n One wants friends and does not know how to start. The other wants a social life and has no time to find it. Both skip events because the first step feels too big.\n Persona 1\n Wants friends, does not know how to start\n Says I want to make new friends but don\u2019t know how.\n I feel nervous about joining events alone.\n I wish I had someone to guide me through campus and city life.\n Feels Lonely and overwhelmed by culture shock.\n Awkward in large groups.\n Anxious about missing out.\n Thinks Cultural differences will make it harder to fit in.\n A buddy system would ease the transition.\n Group study could keep him on track.\n Does Scrolls event boards but rarely attends alone.\n Relies on a few acquaintances or flatmates.\n Skips events without company.\n Persona 2\n Wants a social life, has no time for one\n Says I wish I had a reliable study buddy.\n I don\u2019t have time to explore social activities.\n I need better ways to keep track of events.\n Feels Overwhelmed with coursework.\n Regretful about missing social life.\n Frustrated when events clash or transport fails.\n Thinks Balancing study and social life is impossible.\n Wants networking before graduation.\n Reliable transport would make life easier.\n Does Puts academics ahead of socialising.\n Studies alone, even when demotivated.\n Skips events because logistics feel complicated.\n The journey Low points arrive early, and each one has a feature waiting. \n Stage 1 Arrival & orientation\n Stage 2 Initial socialisation\n Stage 3 Joining activities\n Stage 4 Academic adjustments\n Stage 5 Exploring beyond campus\n Stage 6 Deeper connections\n Feeling\n Excited but overwhelmed, isolated in a new place\n Curious but hesitant to approach others\n Interested but nervous about going without friends\n Stressed and lonely during study hours\n Excited, frustrated by unreliable transport\n Wants closer friends, stuck at small talk\n Pain point\n Does not know anyone; no guidance through culture shock\n Shy; avoids joining events alone\n Cancels if no one comes along; misses out\n No study partner; low motivation\n Shuttle timings unclear; missed buses\n Culture gap and no continuity\n Opportunity\n Newcomer push notifications, buddy system\n Tag-Along to join with others\n Communities of like-minded peers\n Cohorts and study matching\n Heat Map and Shuttle Tracking\n Long-term communities\n 07 \u00b7 Structure Four anchors, everything else one tap away. \n The app is organised around Tag-Along, Communities, Events and the Heat Map. Each answers a specific need: Tag-Along removes going alone, the Heat Map removes not knowing what is on, and shuttle tracking removes the wait.\n Uni-Verse app\n Authentication\n Login Apple, Google, Meta, email \n Sign up Name, email, password \n Home\n Top nav\n Map shortcut\n Events Cards, view all \n Cohorts Cards, view all \n Map (UniMap)\n Search and filter tabs\n Pins Event, cohort, tag-along, shuttle \n Shuttle timings\n Events\n Events main\n Event detail RSVP, info, status \n Event created\n Tag-Along\n Discovery\n Detail Activity info, tag-along button \n Approval Waiting, accepted \n Cohorts\n Communities main Your cohorts, explore \n Cohort detail Info, members, chat, virtual events \n Create\n Tag-Along Activity, vibes, schedule, who can join \n Cohort Name, interests, vibes \n Event Details, rules, RSVP \n Direct messaging\n DM list\n Threads By tag-along, by cohort \n Chat\n Notifications + Profile\n Notifications Tag-along, event, cohort \n Profile User info, connections, interests \n Settings Privacy, prefs, logout \n 08 \u00b7 Design system A dark, calm base with one gradient for energy. \n Black and white neutrals carry the screens. Blue to purple marks what is active, set in SF Pro. Vibe chips such as Chill, Low-key and Team play let students";
